    "image_with_text_gdxHgV": {
      "type": "image-with-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_nGe3fM": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Traction Tape",
            "highlight_style": "none",
            "highlight_font_style": "italic",
            "highlight_style_color": "",
            "highlight_text_color": "",
            "heading_size": "h2"
          }
        },
        "text_P9BrmK": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>As the name suggests, traction tape provides additional traction in workplaces where slips and falls pose a risk. This tape—also called anti-slip tape—has a textured surface that provides additional grip for footwear, hand wear, bare hands, and more. Common locations for traction tape include:</p><ul><li>Near entranceways and exit doors that may have damp surfaces following bad weather</li><li>Around routinely wet or slippery locations, such as showers, locker rooms, or floor drains</li><li>On steps that pose a large safety risk in the event of a slip or fall, even if these steps are not frequently wet or slippery</li><li>Handrails or gripped surfaces in environments where workers wear gloves or potentially slick hand gear</li></ul>",
            "subtext_color": true
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "heading_nGe3fM",
        "text_P9BrmK"
      ],
      "disabled": true,
      "name": "t:sections.image-with-text.name",
